  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt[3]{x-2y}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x-2y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{\u2032}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\sqrt[3]{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{\u2032}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-2\\sqrt[3]{z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=1-2\\sqrt[3]{z}.$",
